## 2. A large potential labor opportunity

We estimate approximately **$5.3tn of U.S. AI-exposed labor income in 2030**, based on employment, wages and occupation-level AI task exposure.

Extending the analysis using relative labor income and differences in sector employment composition suggests a potential AI-exposed wage pool of approximately **$14tn globally excluding China, including the U.S.**

These figures represent the economic scale of tasks potentially affected by AI. They are not estimates of achievable labor savings, employee displacement or AI vendor revenue.

The distinction matters. Technological exposure does not imply that every task will be automated, that every business will adopt AI, or that the full associated wage cost can be eliminated.

To estimate realizable economic value, we adjust the exposed wage pool for the share of economically viable applications, effective AI utilization and potential task-level labor cost savings.

Penn Wharton provides a useful analytical framework, using assumptions for the share of exposed tasks that can be economically automated and the potential cost savings from adopting AI.

Our model applies this framework to the exposed wage pool and tests alternative utilization and productivity scenarios.

