A central consideration is whether the implied subscription price is consistent with the economic value AI generates for customers. If an AI application delivers productivity benefits substantially above its cost, the economic case for adoption strengthens. If pricing approaches or exceeds the value delivered, adoption and monetization may prove more challenging.

## 6. Subscriptions are only part of the monetization opportunity

Our existing enterprise and consumer subscription models provide a foundation for quantifying the more visible AI revenue opportunities.

However, AI monetization may ultimately extend beyond conventional subscription pricing.

AI agents capable of completing multi-step workflows could support usage-based or outcome-based pricing. Industry-specific applications could create value through improved operational efficiency, customer service, software development, healthcare delivery and scientific research.

AI may also enable new services and business models that are difficult to capture using today's market categories.

We therefore distinguish between three types of opportunities:

**Visible monetization:** Enterprise and consumer subscriptions that can be estimated using paying users and subscription pricing.

**Productivity-supported monetization:** Revenue potentially supported by the economic benefits that AI delivers to enterprise customers.

**Emerging revenue opportunities:** New applications, workflows and business models whose ultimate market size and adoption remain uncertain.

These categories should not be treated as automatically additive. Productivity benefits may already underpin subscription willingness to pay, while revenues at different points of the AI value chain can overlap.

Our objective is to estimate the visible revenue base, assess the economic capacity for additional monetization and identify the remaining investment hurdle.

